{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794764019,"nonce":"BoYvwiGEwY5x3N9H4Js8PpCO+cM=","title":"wasm: link ar archives passed with --native-lib","message":"`--native-lib libfoo.a` on a wasm target failed with \"parsing libfoo.a: magic header not detected\": the linker took only plain wasm objects. hxn asked for archive semantics so a Rust static library can be linked into main.wasm instead of loaded as a side module (see c639f0d for what side modules cost on a threaded target: errno, per-thread TLS blocks).\n\nWhat it does now (crates/ash_wasm_link/src/archive.rs, wired in crates/ash/src/llvm/aot_link.rs):\n- reads an ar archive (the `object` crate: GNU long names, BSD names) into wasm objects; non-wasm members are skipped, thin archives refused;\n- pulls a member only when it defines a symbol that something in the link refers to and nothing defines, then searches the pulled members' references in turn; weak references pull nothing;\n- a strong definition in a pulled member that the link already has (runtime, program, an earlier archive) is turned into a reference, so the linker's by-name resolution binds every use to the earlier definition. Plain objects defining one name are still an error. The dropped names are printed;\n- a function a pulled member names that nothing defines and no host supplies (std's `chdir`) is marked weak, so it links as the linker's trapping stub;\n- LinkOptions.prune_imports (set only when an archive is linked) limits imports to what kept code and data reach, because a member's unused functions name things no host supplies;\n- GOT.func: a GlobalIndexLeb relocation against a function symbol is the position-independent way to take its address. Tree shaking now roots it, the function gets a table slot, and a global holds the slot. The existing GOT.mem support covered data only.\n\nChecked:\n- hxn's real libgreeter_hl.a (66 members, build-std) for wasm32-wasip1-threads against the real ash_runtime.o: 49 members pulled, `errno` and `rust_eh_personality` dropped for the runtime's, links to a module that validates with all features. Run with ASH_LINK_TEST_ARCHIVE, ASH_LINK_TEST_RUNTIME, ASH_LINK_TEST_SYMBOL (tests/archives.rs);\n- hermetic tests run the linked module under wasmtime: a member's own call to a name the runtime defines reaches the runtime's, and a function reached only through the GOT lands on the right table slot;\n- a link with no archive is byte-identical to before for a real program on wasm32-wasip1 and wasm32-wasip1-threads.\n\nNot checked, and open:\n- running a program that calls into the linked library. The linked module has not been run under a host with the library's primitives bound; hxn's `links` (AotRequest.links) is the path that binds a native to a symbol, and it has not been exercised against an archive here;\n- the plain CLI binds a library's primitives through slots a side-module loader fills (static_natives is off on wasm), so `ash --build --native-lib libfoo.a` links the library's code but does not call into it by itself;\n- the library's std and the runtime's std both exist in the module when their symbol hashes differ. Only unmangled duplicates (errno, rust_eh_personality, the allocator entry points) bind to the runtime's. If a library's std disagrees with the runtime's about layout, that is where it would show;\n- the build-system choice differs from phones, where ash merges the library into one object that keeps only its C interface global (docs/aot.md). Wasm has no `-r` merge step for this, so it resolves by name at link time.","files":null}]}